A||||25||Pretender||do radar/laser jammers really work?||||1||
Z||000000||Pretender||12-03-1999||04:46 AM||||i just got another speeding ticket (54 in a 25). I've seen articles about the "Phazer" whish is supposed to jam all radar and laser and if you do get cought the'll pay the fine for you. Just wanted to know if anyone has one and if actually works. I think it's like $300 but that is well worth it for me...thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||RFMike||12-03-1999||07:54 AM||||There isn't a short answer to this one.<BR>Radar detectors do detect radar, and good ones detect it at a range that is longer than the reliable radar detection device in the squad car.  The difficulty is in "instant on" radar.  The officer can set up a cone of detection, idle the unit, and laser you when you hit the box.  There is no detection unit that will help you here, unless you pick up a signal when the guy in front of you gets tagged.  Another method with conventional radar is for an officer to follow a semi close and pulled to the right.  This reflects the signal away from oncoming traffic.  When he sees you, he pulls to the right, your detector goes off, and he locks in your speed.  You may slow down slightly, but he can give you a ticket in some states for the maximum observed speed.  (When the Minnesota trooper got me at 86 in a 55 on the Minnesota prairie, he said, "Come with me to the county courthouse.   We only take cash.")<P>In some states, they are illegal.  New police radar can sense radar detectors.  The detector companies have now come out with units that block this detection, but you have to manually activate it.<P>I don't know if the Phaser is a radar jammer.  There are radar jammers available, but they are highly illegal in Minnesota and Wisconsin.  My police friends tell me that they work like this.  The unit can send out a signal that will make the squad car's device read only one speed, such as 57 mph.  The flaw here is that if you are passing someone at 30 mph over the limit, and the officer sees you, he may realize you have a jammer.  Same thing if you are in heavy traffic, and he gets one reading only of 57 mph.<BR>The other approach is to jam the signal.  Obviously, this will make a cop suspiscious.  If you get stopped with one in Wisconsin, the fine is $750 dollars.  Pretty spendy.<BR>I'm not promoting speeding here.  In fact, I think 54 in a 25 (I assume a populated area) is dangerously fast.  The possibility of killing someone is WAY too high.  I think speed is for open spaces.<BR>Be careful.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-03-1999||03:15 PM||||I strongly concur with Mike's last three sentences. It's one thing to be flying low on the open highway, but quite another to being driving over twice the speed limit in a school zone.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||12-03-1999||05:19 PM||||Speaking of jammers.... someone I know had one, and he set it to around 45.  It worked great, until he went through a school zone (and was ACTUALLY doing the limit) and got nailed for 45mph.||||reg||4
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||12-03-1999||06:01 PM||||I've been talking with an electronics guy I know, one idea related to this is an "active stealth" system. Transmit on the police radar frequency band, 180 degrees phase shifted. cancel out your own radar signal.<P>It would need to be combined with a radar detector of course, so it only activates when police radar is detected.||||reg||1
Z||000005||COUNTIBLIS||12-03-1999||06:02 PM||||Like my american express card i dont leave home without my detector.  Mine is cool, it talks to you and tells you what band is scanning.  SInce I got mine I havent had a ticket!  Knock on wood!  But I only speed on the interstate and not through town||||reg||10
Z||000006||theogre||12-03-1999||08:42 PM||||Radar detectors are also banned in Washington DC and Virginia as I recall.<P>Radar "jamming" devices are illegal in all 50 states. These devices are illegal transmitters by FCC definition. There is no such thing as a "passive" jamming device for active radar used in motor vehicle applications. (Dispersing "chaff" is likely to get you stopped.)<P>I don't know if Laser jamming devices are FCC regulated. These would be up to the city/county/state I think. A Laser jamming device has to fire a fairly strong flood of IR energy. It is unlikely most "jammers" i've seen could suficiently flood the lidar gun to keep it from reading. These guns already have to filter out signifigant IR sources such as sunlight.<P>There is not such thing as a stelth car. Radar will bounce off any solid object it hits including your head and other things inside the car.<P>"Laser" guns will read off any reflective surface they hit. Remember that laser is infra red. things you don't think reflect in normal light may reflect IR.<P>Both radar and laser are "dopler shift" measuring devices. you cannot transmit a signal at such a device that will make it read a specific number.<P>BTW license plate covers and frames are illegal in most states, but these laws are rarely enforced.||||reg||1
Z||000007||RFMike||12-03-1999||09:12 PM||||Check this out. <A HREF="http://www.brightfutureinc.com/stealth.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.brightfutureinc.com/stealth.html</A> ||||reg||1
